/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A heat reservoir is a large body whose temperature stays constant when heat enters or leaves it.
   - The first law for one cycle gives W = QH − QC.
   - QH is heat taken from the hot reservoir (kJ); QC is heat rejected to the cold reservoir (kJ); W is work delivered per cycle (kJ); η is thermal efficiency (dimensionless).
   - Engine, refrigerator and heat-pump hardware is in heat engines, refrigerators and heat pumps.
   - Kelvin–Planck statement: no cyclic device can take heat from a single reservoir and convert all of it into work.
   - Clausius statement: no cyclic device can move heat from a colder body to a hotter body without work input.
   - QC is heat removed from the cold space (kJ); QH is heat rejected to the warm space (kJ); Win is work input per cycle (kJ); COPR and COPHP are dimensionless coefficients of performance.
   - Kelvin–Planck and Clausius are equivalent: if one is false, the other is false.
   - Now suppose Clausius is false.
*/
